# ---------------------------------------------------------------- DIASPORA
dict(
slug="afdb-diaspora-initiative-124-billion-remittances",
cats=[8], label="DIASPORA VOICE",
title="AfDB Wants Africa's Diaspora as Investors, Not Just Senders of $124 Billion in Remittances",
excerpt="The African Development Bank has launched a Diaspora Initiative in Paris to channel the skills and savings of Africans abroad into investment at home, as remittances to the continent hit a record $124.2 billion.",
tags=["African Development Bank","Diaspora","Remittances","Investment"],
html="""
<p>Africans living abroad sent home a record <strong>$124.2 billion</strong> in 2025, and the continent's biggest development lender now wants a bigger role for that money than paying school fees and hospital bills.</p>

<p>The African Development Bank Group (AfDB) unveiled a new <strong>Diaspora Initiative</strong> in Paris on Monday, November 9, at a meeting themed "Mobilising diaspora capital for Africa's prosperity and transformation." More than 200 entrepreneurs, private investors, diaspora networks based in Europe and public financial institutions attended, according to the <a href="https://www.afdb.org/en/news-and-events/african-development-bank-unveils-diaspora-98563" target="_blank" rel="noopener">African Development Bank</a>.</p>

<h2>"A strategic partner"</h2>

<p>AfDB President Dr Sidi Ould Tah framed the diaspora as a co-investor rather than an aid channel. "The diaspora is not a beneficiary; it is a strategic partner working towards the continent's prosperity," he said, noting that remittances had passed $124 billion last year (<a href="https://www.afdb.org/en/news-and-events/african-development-bank-unveils-diaspora-98563" target="_blank" rel="noopener">AfDB</a>).</p>

<p>The initiative sits inside the bank's "Four Cardinal Points" strategy and its New African Financial Architecture for Development (NAFAD), which aims to help Africa mobilise and channel its own resources. Didier Acouetey, senior adviser to the AfDB president, described the bank as a "trusted third party that invests," pointing to African savings estimated at more than $4 trillion against annual development financing needs of about $400 billion. He also highlighted the African Trade and Investment Development Insurance (ATIDI) as a tool to reduce the risk of investing in African projects (<a href="https://www.afdb.org/en/news-and-events/african-development-bank-unveils-diaspora-98563" target="_blank" rel="noopener">AfDB</a>).</p>

<p>The African Union's head of citizens and diaspora affairs, Amr Aljowaily, said the plan "comes at just the right time, when Africa must mobilise all its own resources to finance its development," as official development assistance shrinks worldwide.</p>

<h2>The remittance reality</h2>

<p>The scale of the opportunity is clear. Remittances to Africa grew 86% between 2016 and 2025, according to International Fund for Agricultural Development figures cited by <a href="https://www.theafricareport.com/435825/africans-abroad-sent-home-124bn-last-year-and-states-want-a-bigger-share/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">The Africa Report</a>. The same report notes that the inflows are unevenly shared across countries, and that sending money to Africa still costs more than sending it anywhere else in the world.</p>

<p>That cost problem is where fintech comes in. Cheaper transfer rails, stablecoin corridors and diaspora bonds are all being pitched as ways to keep more of each dollar in recipients' hands, and to turn one-off transfers into long-term savings and investment.</p>

<h2>Momentum beyond Paris</h2>

<p>The AfDB launch is part of a wider push. In Lagos, Global Africa Gateway and Afreximbank used the CANEX WKND creative-economy event (November 5 to 8) to connect African creators with diaspora professionals and investors; organisers said more than 15,000 delegates from Africa, the Caribbean and the wider diaspora attended (<a href="https://www.thisdaylive.com/2026/11/13/global-africa-gateway-advances-diaspora-partnerships-at-2026-canex-wknd/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">ThisDay</a>). In Ghana, officials have been urged to remove practical barriers to permanent return for people of African descent (<a href="https://allafrica.com/stories/202611050657.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener">allAfrica</a>).</p>

<p>For Africans abroad, the message from Paris is that the continent's lenders want them at the table as shareholders and bondholders, not just as the family member who sends money at the end of the month. Whether the new instruments are cheaper, safer and easier to access than a mobile money transfer will decide if that pitch works.</p>
"""),

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- FINANCE
dict(
slug="nigeria-proposes-west-african-capital-market-passport",
cats=[15], label="FINANCE",
title="Nigeria Proposes a 'Passport' to Link 10 West African Capital Markets",
excerpt="Finance Minister Taiwo Oyedele wants issuers approved in one West African market to raise money across the region, with trades settled in local currencies instead of the US dollar.",
tags=["Nigeria","Capital Markets","Taiwo Oyedele","ECOWAS","PAPSS"],
html="""
<p>Nigeria has proposed connecting the capital markets of 10 West African countries so that companies and governments can raise money across the region without starting the approval process from scratch in each country.</p>

<p>Finance Minister and Coordinating Minister of the Economy <strong>Taiwo Oyedele</strong> set out the plan on Tuesday, November 10, at the S&amp;P Global Ratings Africa Summit in Lagos. He argued that fragmented markets were holding the region back, with no single exchange deep enough to attract the scale of long-term investment needed for infrastructure, according to <a href="https://www.theeastafrican.co.ke/tea/business-tech/nigeria-proposes-regional-bond-market-reforms-5666842" target="_blank" rel="noopener">The EastAfrican</a>.</p>

<h2>What is on the table</h2>

<ul>
<li><strong>A regional passport:</strong> an issuer approved in one participating market could raise capital in the others without separate regulatory approvals.</li>
<li><strong>One investor ID:</strong> investors registered in one country could buy bonds and equities across the region without repeating onboarding.</li>
<li><strong>Local-currency settlement:</strong> linked trading, settlement and payment systems so securities deals settle directly in local currencies, reducing reliance on the US dollar.</li>
<li><strong>New tools:</strong> a regional local-currency bond index and a credit enhancement facility for cross-border infrastructure.</li>
</ul>

<p>The plan would cover Nigeria, Ghana and the eight CFA franc countries that share a common central bank: Benin, Burkina Faso, Côte d'Ivoire, Guinea-Bissau, Mali, Niger, Senegal and Togo (<a href="https://www.theeastafrican.co.ke/tea/business-tech/nigeria-proposes-regional-bond-market-reforms-5666842" target="_blank" rel="noopener">The EastAfrican</a>).</p>

<h2>"Pilots, not treaties"</h2>

<p>Oyedele said regulators should start small, with a pilot covering Nigeria, Ghana and the BRVM, the regional exchange of the French-speaking CFA countries. "Let us start with pilots, not treaties," he said, calling for a coalition of willing regulators, one passport and one cross-border bond within 12 months, as reported by <a href="https://moneycentral.com.ng/markets/article/oyedele-proposes-west-african-capital-market-passport-sets-three-year-investment-grade-goal/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">MoneyCentral</a>.</p>

<p>He pointed to the West African Capital Markets Integration Council, the African Exchanges Linkage Project and the Pan-African Payment and Settlement System (PAPSS) as existing building blocks. "The task now is to join these pieces together," he said.</p>

<p>The minister also restated Nigeria's ambition to win an investment-grade credit rating within three years, after S&amp;P upgraded the country to B with a stable outlook in May, and to build a capital market worth at least $1 trillion under a 10-year plan (<a href="https://moneycentral.com.ng/markets/article/oyedele-proposes-west-african-capital-market-passport-sets-three-year-investment-grade-goal/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">MoneyCentral</a>; <a href="https://www.taxnigeria.com/news/fg-proposes-west-african-capital-market-reforms-to-ease-cross-border-investment" target="_blank" rel="noopener">TaxNigeria</a>).</p>

<h2>Trade finance boost</h2>

<p>The proposal landed the same week the African Development Bank announced a $200 million revolving trade-finance line of credit for the Trade and Development Bank (TDB). The facility, approved on October 28, is expected to support more than $1 billion in trade transactions over three and a half years (<a href="https://africaports.co.za/2026/11/13/africa-ports-ships-maritimne-news-8-9-november-2026/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Africa Ports &amp; Ships</a>).</p>

<h2>What happens next</h2>

<p>For now, the regional framework is a proposal with no implementation timetable. Its success depends on regulators in 10 countries, two currency zones and different legal traditions agreeing on common rules. If it works, a saver in Accra or Dakar could one day buy a Nigerian infrastructure bond as easily as a local one, and settle the trade without touching the dollar.</p>
"""),

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- CRYPTO
dict(
slug="bitcoin-falls-below-69000-liquidations-extreme-fear",
cats=[14], label="CRYPTO NEWS",
title="Bitcoin Slides Toward $65,000 as $1.4 Billion in Liquidations Push Crypto Into 'Extreme Fear'",
excerpt="Bitcoin has lost about 14% in a week, hitting its lowest level since August as leveraged bets unwound, ETF outflows mounted and traders priced in another Federal Reserve rate hike.",
tags=["Bitcoin","Ethereum","Liquidations","Bitcoin ETF","Federal Reserve"],
html="""
<p>Bitcoin's slide deepened this week, with the world's largest cryptocurrency breaking below $70,000 on Friday and trading near <strong>$65,200</strong> on Saturday morning, about 14% lower than a week ago, according to <a href="https://bitcoinmagazine.com/markets/bitcoin-price-crashes-13-percent-65600" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Bitcoin Magazine</a>.</p>

<p>On Friday, bitcoin fell 7% in 24 hours to $68,408, its lowest level since August 20, while ether dropped 6% to $1,921, BNB fell 6.3% and XRP lost 3.5%, <a href="https://www.theblock.co/news/markets/2026-11-13-bitcoin-tumbles-below-69000-439643" target="_blank" rel="noopener">The Block</a> reported.</p>

<h2>A leverage flush</h2>

<p>Most of the damage came from forced selling. Crypto liquidations totalled about $1.47 billion over 24 hours, including $1.31 billion in long positions betting on higher prices, with more than 175,000 traders liquidated, according to CoinGlass data cited by <a href="https://www.theblock.co/news/markets/2026-11-13-bitcoin-tumbles-below-69000-439643" target="_blank" rel="noopener">The Block</a>.</p>

<p>"The sharp leg lower in crypto looks like a leverage event as BTC broke key levels," said Rick Maeda, head of markets at Laevitas. Once bitcoin broke $70,000, he said, long positions clustered below that level "were forced out quickly."</p>

<h2>Why the market is under pressure</h2>

<ul>
<li><strong>ETF outflows:</strong> BlackRock's IBIT recorded more than $2 billion in net outflows over nine straight sessions, and nearly $1.44 billion left US spot bitcoin ETFs over five sessions, according to Cointelegraph figures cited by <a href="https://coinmarketcap.com/community/articles/6af6c256c2b46320566ca117/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">CoinMarketCap</a>.</li>
<li><strong>Rate-hike fears:</strong> a hotter-than-expected October CPI reading raised the odds of a Federal Reserve rate hike in December, the US Dollar Index rose above 103, and the 10-year Treasury yield climbed above 5% (<a href="https://coinmarketcap.com/community/articles/6af6c256c2b46320566ca117/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">CoinMarketCap</a>).</li>
<li><strong>Tech turmoil:</strong> traders were also digesting the leadership shake-up at Nvidia after co-founder Jensen Huang resigned as CEO, which weighed on risk assets broadly (<a href="https://www.theblock.co/news/markets/2026-11-13-bitcoin-tumbles-below-69000-439643" target="_blank" rel="noopener">The Block</a>).</li>
</ul>

<p>Total crypto market capitalisation fell about 7% in a day to roughly $2.35 trillion, and the Crypto Fear &amp; Greed Index dropped to 20, its first "Extreme Fear" reading since July (<a href="https://finance.yahoo.com/markets/crypto/articles/bitcoin-price-crashes-below-69-095953166.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Yahoo Finance</a>).</p>

<h2>What traders are watching</h2>

<p>Analysts are focused on US inflation data and the Fed's December 9 meeting. BTSE COO Jeff Mei said a 50-basis-point hike instead of the expected 25 could give crypto "more room to fall." ViaBTC chief analyst Jeff Ko offered a counterpoint: persistent inflation could strengthen bitcoin's appeal as a hedge over the longer term (<a href="https://www.theblock.co/news/markets/2026-11-13-bitcoin-tumbles-below-69000-439643" target="_blank" rel="noopener">The Block</a>). CoinDesk has flagged a turning momentum gauge that, if history repeats, could take bitcoin back below $60,000 (<a href="https://www.coindesk.com/daybook-us/2026/11/13/bitcoin-s-momentum-gauge-is-turning-red-could-send-prices-back-below-usd60-000" target="_blank" rel="noopener">CoinDesk</a>).</p>

<h2>What it means for African users</h2>

<p>For traders in Lagos, Nairobi and Johannesburg who use crypto for savings and cross-border payments, the week is a reminder that volatility cuts both ways. Leverage magnifies losses, and stablecoin balances, not bitcoin, remain the safer option for anyone who needs to pay bills in the short term.</p>

<p><em>This article is for information only and is not investment advice.</em></p>
"""),

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- BUSINESS
dict(
slug="opay-304-million-nyse-ipo-halted-what-we-know",
cats=[220], label="BUSINESS",
title="OPay's $304 Million New York IPO: What We Know Two Weeks After It Was Pulled",
excerpt="Nigeria's biggest payments app rang the NYSE bell, then cancelled its listing hours later over an unnamed foreign regulatory inquiry. Reports now point to China, and Nigerian lawmakers want federal intervention.",
tags=["OPay","IPO","NYSE","Nigeria Fintech","SoftBank"],
html="""
<p>Two weeks after OPay abandoned what would have been one of the largest US listings by an African fintech, the Nigerian payments company still has not named the regulator that derailed the deal, and pressure is building at home for answers.</p>

<h2>What happened</h2>

<p>OPay priced 16 million American depositary shares at $19 each on Friday, October 30, raising about $304 million and valuing the company at roughly $4 billion. Its executives rang the opening bell at the New York Stock Exchange that day. But the NYSE halted the stock at about 3:30 p.m. pending news, and not a single share under the ticker OPAY ever traded (<a href="https://cioafrica.co/opay-pulls-304m-new-york-ipo-hours-after-pricing/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">CIO Africa</a>; <a href="https://asiatimes.com/2026/11/chinese-regulatory-shadow-hangs-over-nigerias-opay-ipo-failure/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Asia Times</a>).</p>

<p>In a statement, the SoftBank-backed company said it and its underwriters had "mutually agreed to terminate" their October 29 underwriting agreement because of "a regulatory inquiry relating to a jurisdiction immaterial to the Company's business operations." OPay said "not proceeding with the offering at this time would be the most prudent course of action" and that it was "confident that the inquiry will be concluded satisfactorily in the near future" (<a href="https://cioafrica.co/opay-pulls-304m-new-york-ipo-hours-after-pricing/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">CIO Africa</a>).</p>

<h2>The China question</h2>

<p>On November 4, Bloomberg reported that inquiries from China's securities regulator prompted the halt, citing people familiar with the matter. Those people said OPay has no operations or revenue in China and that its link to the country is its founder, Chinese billionaire Zhou Yahui (<a href="https://news.bloomberglaw.com/mergers-and-acquisitions/africa-focused-opay-is-said-to-halt-us-ipo-after-china-inquiry" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Bloomberg Law</a>). OPay has not confirmed this.</p>

<p>The prospectus had addressed the issue directly. OPay said it did not need approval from the China Securities Regulatory Commission (CSRC) under China's 2023 overseas listing rules because it earned no revenue in mainland China in 2025 and its assets, operations and senior managers are outside the mainland, as <a href="https://techcabal.com/2026/11/05/opay-china-overseas-listing-rules-us-ipo/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">TechCabal</a> detailed. It also acknowledged uncertainty over how those rules could be applied.</p>

<h2>Nigerian lawmakers step in</h2>

<p>Nigeria's House of Representatives has called for federal intervention, with lawmakers describing "regulatory complications arising from claims about OPay's alleged Chinese corporate status," according to <a href="https://www.intellinews.com/nigerian-lawmakers-urge-federal-intervention-over-opay-s-halted-304mn-new-york-ipo-484609/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">bne IntelliNews</a>. Talks between OPay and the CSRC were reported to be continuing.</p>

<h2>What it means for customers</h2>

<p>OPay has said the inquiry has no impact on its operations, regulatory standing or services in Nigeria or its other markets. An inquiry does not establish wrongdoing, and the halted IPO is not a sign of an operational failure. For the millions of Nigerians who use OPay for transfers, bills and POS cash, the app works as before.</p>

<p>For African tech more broadly, the episode is a warning. Founders and investors with ties to China now face questions about whether Beijing's overseas listing rules can reach companies that do no business there. OPay has given no new timeline for another listing attempt.</p>
"""),

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- SECURITY
dict(
slug="maersk-legacy-system-data-theft-whitearmor-cisa-kev",
cats=[17], label="SECURITY NEWS",
title="Maersk Confirms Data Theft From Legacy System as Ransomware Group Demands $5 Million",
excerpt="The shipping giant says an inherited system was breached but bookings were not affected. Meanwhile, CISA flagged six actively exploited flaws, and attackers are hijacking remote-management tools used by IT providers.",
tags=["Maersk","Ransomware","CISA","Cybersecurity","Data Breach"],
html="""
<p>Shipping giant <strong>Maersk</strong>, whose vessels and terminals handle a large share of Africa's container trade, has confirmed that an unauthorised party accessed and extracted data from a local legacy system it inherited through an earlier acquisition.</p>

<p>Maersk said its investigation so far "strongly suggests" no sensitive information was disclosed, and that the incident was isolated to that one system. The company reported no impact on its other systems, on customer and partner integrations such as APIs and EDI, or on shipment bookings, according to the <a href="https://www.cybersecbrief.com/news/cybersec/cybersec-2026-11-12" target="_blank" rel="noopener">CyberSecBrief daily briefing</a>.</p>

<p>A group calling itself <strong>WhiteArmor</strong> is demanding $5 million, BleepingComputer reported. Maersk has not confirmed an extortion demand or said what data was taken, so the claim remains unverified (<a href="https://www.cybersecbrief.com/news/cybersec/cybersec-2026-11-12" target="_blank" rel="noopener">CyberSecBrief</a>).</p>

<h2>Why old systems matter</h2>

<p>The Maersk incident highlights a common weak spot: systems that come with an acquisition and are never fully absorbed into the parent company's security controls. For African banks, fintechs and telecoms that grow by buying smaller rivals, the lesson is to inventory and secure inherited systems quickly, or shut them down.</p>

<h2>Six flaws under active attack</h2>

<p>The US Cybersecurity and Infrastructure Security Agency (CISA) added six vulnerabilities to its Known Exploited Vulnerabilities catalogue this week, based on evidence of active exploitation. They affect Cisco BroadWorks, Barracuda products including Email Security Gateway, Openwave Messaging Webtop Media Transcoder, Microsoft Outlook and Google Chromium (<a href="https://www.cybersecbrief.com/news/cybersec/cybersec-2026-11-12" target="_blank" rel="noopener">CyberSecBrief</a>). Organisations using these products should patch as a priority.</p>

<h2>IT providers in the crosshairs</h2>

<ul>
<li><strong>ScreenConnect:</strong> ConnectWise is investigating a campaign in which compromised ScreenConnect environments were used to push rogue remote-access clients to customer devices, affecting managed service providers and dozens of organisations.</li>
<li><strong>N-able:</strong> the company released hotfixes for its N-central remote monitoring platform after zero-day flaws were disclosed publicly, forcing it to take vulnerable hosted environments offline.</li>
<li><strong>Stolen AI keys:</strong> Railway said AI provider keys stored by customers were stolen in an October breach and used to run up an estimated $740,000 in unauthorised charges.</li>
</ul>

<p>All three were reported by <a href="https://www.cybersecbrief.com/news/cybersec/cybersec-2026-11-12" target="_blank" rel="noopener">CyberSecBrief</a>.</p>

<h2>What to do now</h2>

<p>Security teams should patch the CISA-listed flaws, review who has remote-management access to their networks, rotate any API keys stored on third-party platforms, and set spending limits on AI and cloud accounts. Small businesses that rely on an outside IT provider should ask that provider directly whether it uses ScreenConnect or N-central and what it has done this week.</p>
"""),

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- AI
dict(
slug="openai-chatgpt-wallet-ai-agents-pay-visa-mastercard-amex",
cats=[2], label="ARTIFICIAL INTELLIGENCE",
title="OpenAI Launches ChatGPT Wallet, Letting AI Agents Pay for You Without Seeing Your Card",
excerpt="Built with American Express, Mastercard and Visa, ChatGPT Wallet lets OpenAI's 'dots' agents complete purchases using one-time payment tokens, with a Face ID or fingerprint check for every transaction.",
tags=["OpenAI","ChatGPT Wallet","Agentic Commerce","Visa","Mastercard","American Express"],
html="""
<p>OpenAI has launched <strong>ChatGPT Wallet</strong>, a payment feature that lets its AI agents buy things on a user's behalf without ever seeing the user's actual card details.</p>

<p>Announced on Friday, November 13, the wallet was built with <strong>American Express, Mastercard and Visa</strong>. OpenAI says it is the first wallet that lets agents pay without access to real card numbers and that requires a security check for every purchase, while keeping existing card rewards and dispute protections (<a href="http://openai.com/index/introducing-chatgpt-wallet" target="_blank" rel="noopener">OpenAI</a>).</p>

<h2>How it works</h2>

<ol>
<li>A user adds an eligible card once.</li>
<li>They ask their "dot," OpenAI's always-on personal agent, to find and buy something, such as a sweater or a hotel booking.</li>
<li>Before paying, the user reviews the merchant, items, total and selected card, then approves with Face ID, Touch ID or a fingerprint scan.</li>
<li>The card network issues a <strong>payment token</strong>, a substitute card number, security code and expiry date, so neither the agent nor the merchant sees the real card.</li>
</ol>

<p>That flow is described in <a href="https://help.openai.com/en/articles/20001654-making-purchases-with-chatgpt-wallet" target="_blank" rel="noopener">OpenAI's help centre</a>, which also warns that some websites may not support agent purchases and that users should check the merchant's order confirmation.</p>

<h2>Who can use it</h2>

<p>The rollout is narrow. ChatGPT Wallet is available only to ChatGPT Pro subscribers aged 18 or older in the United States, with an eligible US-issued American Express, Chase, Mastercard or Visa card and a US billing address. It works only in conversations with a dot, not in regular ChatGPT chats, and virtual cards are not supported at launch (<a href="https://help.openai.com/en/articles/20001654-making-purchases-with-chatgpt-wallet" target="_blank" rel="noopener">OpenAI Help Center</a>).</p>

<p>OpenAI is not taking a cut of transactions for now, <a href="https://www.cnbc.com/2026/11/13/openai-chatgpt-wallet-dot-purchases.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener">CNBC</a> reported, though agent-driven shopping could become a new revenue stream. Amex's Luke Gebb told CNBC the limited rollout is the "right approach to make sure things are working."</p>

<h2>What comes next</h2>

<p>OpenAI expects users eventually to pre-authorise agents to buy within set limits, such as spending caps, product categories or time periods, but those features are not part of the first release (<a href="https://www.reuters.com/technology/openai-launches-chatgpt-wallet-letting-ai-agents-pay-purchases-2026-11-13/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Reuters</a>). Merchants that want to accept agent payments will need to make sure their sites do not block AI agents and that their fraud systems can recognise legitimate agent purchases (<a href="https://tech.yahoo.com/ai/chatgpt/articles/openai-introduces-chatgpt-wallet-visa-162102250.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Yahoo Tech</a>).</p>

<h2>The Africa angle</h2>

<p>ChatGPT Wallet is US-only for now, but the model it uses, tokenised cards plus biometric approval, is already familiar to African fintechs building on Visa and Mastercard rails. The bigger question for the continent is whether agentic commerce will plug into mobile money and instant-payment systems like Nigeria's NIP, Kenya's M-Pesa and PAPSS, where most everyday payments actually happen. Whoever builds that bridge first could own the next wave of digital commerce in Africa.</p>
"""),

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- SPORTS
dict(
slug="afcon-2027-qualifiers-nigeria-beat-tanzania-arokodare",
cats=[7], label="SPORTS",
title="AFCON 2027 Qualifiers: Arokodare Brace Lifts Super Eagles Past Tanzania as Ghana End Winless Run",
excerpt="Nigeria beat co-hosts Tanzania 3-1 in Uyo, Ghana ended a 37-month winless streak, and Morocco and Egypt both scored five on a busy matchday three.",
tags=["AFCON 2027","Super Eagles","Tolu Arokodare","Ghana Black Stars","Morocco","Flamingos"],
html="""
<p>Nigeria's Super Eagles kept their 2027 Africa Cup of Nations hopes on track with a <strong>3-1 win over Tanzania</strong> on Friday, November 13, at the Godswill Akpabio International Stadium in Uyo.</p>

<p>Alhassan Yusuf put the hosts ahead early, but Tanzania, co-hosts of the 2027 tournament, levelled through Alphonce Msanga in the 32nd minute. The Super Eagles had to wait until the second half to take control, when a change from the bench turned the game, <a href="https://www.beinsports.com/en-us/soccer/africa-cup-of-nations-qualification/articles-video/nigeria-vs-tanzania-highlights-afcon-2027-qualifiers-2026-11-13" target="_blank" rel="noopener">beIN Sports</a> reported. <strong>Tolu Arokodare</strong> scored twice to seal the points (<a href="https://www.legit.ng/sports/football/1741234-tolu-arokodare-uplifts-super-eagles-crucial-afcon-2027-qualifier-win-tanzania/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Legit.ng</a>).</p>

<p>The win was badly needed after a 3-0 defeat away to Guinea-Bissau in the previous round (<a href="https://allafrica.com/stories/202611120395.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener">allAfrica</a>). It moves Eric Chelle's side to second in Group L, one point behind Guinea-Bissau, who were held 1-1 by Madagascar (<a href="https://www.legit.ng/sports/football/1741234-tolu-arokodare-uplifts-super-eagles-crucial-afcon-2027-qualifier-win-tanzania/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Legit.ng</a>). The return leg is on Tuesday, November 17, at the Amaan Stadium in Zanzibar, kicking off at 2 p.m. WAT (<a href="https://www.afrik-foot.com/en-ng/super-eagles-fixtures-and-results" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Afrik-Foot</a>).</p>

<h2>Around the continent</h2>

<ul>
<li><strong>Ghana 2-0 Somalia:</strong> the four-time champions ended a 37-month winless run in AFCON qualifiers with victory in Essipong on Thursday (<a href="https://www.nampa.org/text/23065025" target="_blank" rel="noopener">NAMPA/AFP</a>).</li>
<li><strong>Morocco 5-1 Niger:</strong> Achraf Hakimi scored twice in Casablanca, with Zabiri, En-Nesyri and Oufkir also on target (<a href="https://www.nampa.org/text/23065882" target="_blank" rel="noopener">NAMPA/AFP</a>).</li>
<li><strong>Egypt 5-0 Malawi:</strong> Omar Marmoush and Mohamed Salah were among the scorers in Cairo, Salah netting twice late on (<a href="https://www.nampa.org/text/23065882" target="_blank" rel="noopener">NAMPA/AFP</a>).</li>
<li><strong>Gabon 2-0 Lesotho:</strong> Denis Bouanga scored both goals in Franceville (<a href="https://www.nampa.org/text/23065882" target="_blank" rel="noopener">NAMPA/AFP</a>).</li>
<li><strong>Senegal 4-1 Sudan:</strong> the Lions came from 1-1 at half-time in Dakar, with debutant Pathé Mboup scoring with his first touch (<a href="https://www.panafricanewsmedia.com/sport/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Pan African News Media</a>).</li>
<li><strong>DR Congo 3-1 Sierra Leone:</strong> the Leopards kept their perfect qualifying record (<a href="https://www.panafricanewsmedia.com/sport/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Pan African News Media</a>).</li>
</ul>

<p>Teams play two matches between November 12 and 17 in this fourth window, which could reveal the first clear trends before the final two matchdays (<a href="https://sportnewsafrica.com/en/index.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Sport News Africa</a>).</p>

<h2>Flamingos still flying</h2>

<p>The week's results come days after Nigeria's <strong>Flamingos</strong> made history in Morocco, beating Japan 2-1 in the FIFA U-17 Women's World Cup final on November 7. Goals from Awawu Bashiru (39th minute) and Oluebube Umejiaku (47th) made Nigeria the first African team to win the tournament and the first African women's team to win a FIFA World Cup at any level (<a href="https://www.channelstv.com/2026/11/13/how-flamingos-ended-africas-long-wait-for-womens-world-cup-glory/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Channels TV</a>). Nigeria is also now the first African country to win the U-17 World Cup in both the men's and women's categories (<a href="https://www.pulse.com.gh/story/nigeria-japan-u17-womens-world-cup-final-2026-2026110810382047998" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Pulse Ghana</a>).</p>
"""),

